r of hits including The  Fast and the Furious and xxx  but his box-office streak took a serious  nosedive when his aviation actioner  stealth crashed and burned at the box  office the film followed a crew of Air  Force pilots trying to control an  artificial intelligence jet but instead  of being a compelling adventure movie it  was more like a science-fiction parody  of Top Gun it grossed just 76 million  dollars against a  135 million dollar budget and made good  on its title by sneaking away from the  theatres largely unnoticed Sahara based  on a massively successful series of  books about a rugged adventurer with a  quick wit and a way with women  Sahara looked like it might be able to  recapture the magic of Indiana Jones  unfortunately for everyone involved the  built-in audience for the books didn't  translate to a box office success and  Sahara grossed a modest 119 point three  million dollars that wasn't enough to  cover its 160 million dollar budget and  81 million dollars in distribution costs  part of it could have just been poor  timing since the movie starred Matthew  McConaughey before his star power  returned in full force with the mechonis  Anse  be a lot cooler if you did but either  way the studio was parched after this  movie's returns Mars Needs Moms Disney's  experiment with motion capture  performance co-produced by legendary  director Robert Zemeckis seemed like a  good idea on paper and Mars Needs Moms  wasn't conceptually off the mark either  as it followed a nine-year-old trying to  save his mom from aliens who wanted to  harness her powers to juice up their  nanny BOTS which is the stuff of a  fairly standard family fare but while  the film's effects were cutting-edge  they gave an almost uncomfortable  eeriness to every characters facial  expressions and rather than an exciting  adventure or a surreal nightmare of a  film the movie ended up being a snooze  audiences on earth didn't have much  interest in it handing over just 39  million 